window.EN = {
  "meta.title": "How to lower your cortisol (and whether you should)",
  "top.home": "Renato Shirakashi",
  "top.kicker": "Cortisol · 2026",
  "hero.eyebrow": "Interactive essay · November 2026",
  "hero.h1": "\n    <span class=\"mark\">How to lower your cortisol</span>\n    <span class=\"mark l2\">(and whether you should)</span>\n  ",
  "hero.sub": "Cortisol helps you wake up, puts sugar into the blood and readies the body for the day’s challenges. It runs high in the morning and falls until midnight, and sleep, light, coffee, exercise and stress all shift that rise and fall. In the studies, people whose cortisol is almost as high at night as in the morning get sick and die more often.",
  "hero.byline": "by Renato Shirakashi",
  "s00.step": "00 · The fad",
  "s00.h": "Searches for cortisol hit a record in 2026, driven by a meme",
  "s00.p1": "On February 12, 2026, a TikTok account posted a few-second edit in which Agnes Tachyon, a character from the Japanese game <em>Uma Musume</em>, dances to the Vietnamese song “Ai Đưa Em Về”. The video became known as the “Low Cortisol Dance” and had 2.8 million views in two weeks. In social-media slang, “low cortisol” became the name of an aesthetic: videos that cut from a tense scene to a slow life with calm music. In March, a month after the video, worldwide Google searches for “cortisol” reached the highest value in the series. The fastest-rising queries were “low cortisol meme”, “high cortisol meme”, “low cortisol dance” and “low cortisol song”, and at the top of the list was “what is <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cortisol\">cortisol</span>”.",
  "s00.p2": "The wave began two years earlier, with a drink. The “cortisol cocktail”, made of orange juice, coconut water and salt, had its search peak in April 2024, and “cortisol face”, the idea that stress puffs up the face, peaked in August 2024 and in May 2026. In Brazil, the December 2024 wave came with searches for “cortisol detox”. Since September 2025, people in the United States can also measure cortisol at home with Eli Health’s Hormometer, which reads a saliva sample with a phone camera and costs from $8.25 per test.",
  "s00.p3": "Searches for cortisol overtook searches for dopamine, the subject of the previous essay in this series. Worldwide, cortisol has been ahead every month since February 2023, and in Brazil the crossover came later, in October 2024; in 2026, Brazilian searches for cortisol were almost triple those for dopamine:",
  "c00.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Cortisol and dopamine on Google, month by month, 2018 to 2026</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the two panels, in which the boxes at the peaks say what people were searching for that month.</span>",
  "c00.cap": "Google Trends, joint queries of 11/12/2026 (pytrends). The index runs from 0 to 100, and 100 is the month with the most searches within each panel; the two panels cannot be compared with each other.",
  "s00.p4": "Most of the demand is for “high cortisol”, “what is cortisol” and “cortisol test”, and in Brazil searches for “high cortisol” grew sevenfold between 2018 and 2026. The question behind them has two parts, what lowers cortisol and whether anyone should want to lower it, and both answers depend on what it does in the body.",
  "s01.step": "01 · What it is for",
  "s01.h": "Cortisol puts sugar into the blood, holds up blood pressure and damps down inflammation, and it rises with every challenge",
  "s01.p1": "Cortisol is a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hormonio\">hormone</span> that helps you wake up, puts sugar into the blood for the muscles and the brain, holds up blood pressure and damps down <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"inflamacao\">inflammation</span>. Faced with a challenge, such as an exam, a race or an argument, production rises and puts more sugar into the blood for the effort. Cortisol is made by the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"adrenais\">adrenal glands</span>, two small glands that sit on top of the kidneys.",
  "s01.p2": "The brain controls production. The <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hipotalamo\">hypothalamus</span> releases a hormone that stimulates the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hipofise\">pituitary</span>, just below it, and the pituitary releases another hormone, which reaches the adrenals through the blood and makes them produce cortisol. When cortisol in the blood rises, it <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"retro\">inhibits the hypothalamus and the pituitary</span>, and production falls. Because production depends on these <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"eixo\">signals from the brain</span>, it changes with what the brain registers through the day: light and sleep times, food and fasting, physical effort and threatening situations, especially when other people judge what the person does. That is why the hours at which a person sleeps, eats, exercises and gets stressed change when and how much cortisol the adrenals release.",
  "s01.p3": "The body makes little cortisol, and it does not last long in the blood. Measured with an isotope tracer in 12 volunteers, production was 9.9 mg a day (Esteban and colleagues, 1991), one fiftieth of a 500 mg painkiller tablet. In four men given cortisol into a vein, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"meiavida\">half the dose left the blood</span> in about 66 minutes (Kraan and colleagues, 1997), which is why the level rises and falls several times in a single day. The drawing below shows what it does in each part of the body:",
  "c01.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What cortisol does in each part of the body</b><span class=\"wdo\">Follow the lines from the body to each box, and in the bottom strip, see what happens when cortisol is missing and when there is too much for months.</span>",
  "c01.cap": "Textbook endocrinology functions; Husebye et al. (<em>Lancet</em>, 2021); Adam et al. (<em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em>, 2017), who propose the clock role; Esteban et al. (<em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em>, 1991); Lupien et al. (<em>Brain Cogn</em>, 2007).",
  "s01.p4": "The effect on inflammation gave cortisol a career as a drug. In September 1948, at the Mayo Clinic, the physician Philip Hench gave <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cortisona\">cortisone</span>, a form of cortisol that the body activates, to a 29-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis, and by the third day she had few symptoms. Two years later, Hench, Edward Kendall and Tadeus Reichstein shared the Nobel Prize in Medicine. Pharmacy <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"corticoide\">corticosteroids</span>, such as <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"prednisona\">prednisone</span>, descend from that line.",
  "s01.p5": "The cortisol the videos want to lower is the same cortisol that puts sugar into the blood in the morning and during an exam, so whether it makes sense to lower it depends on the hour at which it is high, and by how much.",
  "s02.step": "02 · It follows a clock",
  "s02.h": "Cortisol rises just after waking and falls about eightfold by midnight",
  "s02.p1": "Before you see the measured curve, draw your own on the axis below, which runs from 6 a.m. to 6 a.m. the next day. Trace with your finger or the mouse where you think cortisol rises and where it falls:",
  "c02a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Draw a day of cortisol</b><span class=\"wdo\">Trace the line from left to right, from 6 a.m. to 6 a.m.; when you let go, the measured curve appears over yours.</span>",
  "c02a.cap": "Debono et al. (<em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em>, 2009), 24-hour profiles in 33 healthy volunteers. Vermilion marks more cortisol and teal marks less; the colors do not mean good or bad. The smooth curve is an average and hides pulses of about an hour.",
  "s02.p2": "The measured curve comes from 33 healthy volunteers whose blood was drawn over 24 hours (Debono and colleagues, 2009). The average peak was 15.5 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"unidades\">µg/dL</span> at 8:32 a.m., and the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"vale\">trough</span> was below 2 µg/dL at 12:18 a.m., about an eighth of the peak, in a rise and fall that repeats every day. From 7:43 p.m. to 5:31 a.m., cortisol stayed below the day’s average, and the morning rise began around 2 or 3 a.m., while the person was still asleep. If your drawing put the peak in the late afternoon, after work, it treated cortisol as a record of accumulated tiredness. The body does the opposite and puts cortisol at its highest as the day begins.",
  "s02.p3": "The biggest jump comes in the first half hour after waking. In healthy adults, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"livre\">saliva cortisol</span> rises by 50% to 160% in that interval (Clow and colleagues, 2004), to 1.5 to 2.6 times the value at the moment the person opened their eyes. In a study of 509 adults, the average rise was about 50%, and about three in four people showed the rise on a given morning (Wüst and colleagues, 2000). Fries and colleagues (2009) propose that the jump is bigger on mornings when the person expects a demanding day. The chart below zooms in on that first hour:",
  "c02b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The first hour after waking</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the whole day, on top, with the first hour enlarged below, and look at the band of the rise and at the four mornings at the end.</span>",
  "c02b.cap": "Stalder et al. (<em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em>, 2016), expert consensus on the cortisol <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"car\">awakening response</span>; Clow et al. (<em>Stress</em>, 2004); Wüst et al. (2000), 509 adults. Free cortisol in saliva.",
  "s02.p4": "The rise is so fast that the time of collection changes a test result, and delaying the first saliva sample by 5 to 15 minutes already distorts the measurement (Stalder and colleagues, 2016).",
  "s02.p5": "The smooth curve is also an average, because the adrenals release cortisol in pulses of about an hour, and the day’s curve is the height of those pulses rising and falling (Spiga and colleagues, 2014). The habits in the next section act on this clock, and the effect of each depends on the hour at which it happens; the same bright light that raises cortisol in the morning, for example, changes nothing in the afternoon.",
  "s03.step": "03 · Lifestyle",
  "s03.h": "Light, coffee and exercise raise cortisol for a few hours, and short sleep leaves it up to 45% higher the next evening",
  "s03.p1": "Bright morning light raises the cortisol peak. In one experiment, 5,000 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"lux\">lux</span> from 5 to 8 a.m. raised cortisol by 50% compared with dim light, and 800 lux raised it by 35%, while the same light in the afternoon changed nothing (Leproult and colleagues, 2001). <span class=\"mark\">The videos that tell you to get sunlight first thing are telling you to raise the cortisol that other videos tell you to lower.</span>",
  "s03.p2": "The effect of coffee depends on habit. In 2005, William Lovallo and colleagues gave caffeine or <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"placebo\">placebo</span> to 96 adults for four weeks, in a trial in which neither the participants nor the researchers knew who was taking what. After five days without caffeine, three doses of 250 mg (at 9 a.m., 1 p.m. and 6 p.m.) raised cortisol throughout the day. After five days of 300 or 600 mg a day, the 9 a.m. dose no longer raised cortisol, and the 1 p.m. dose still raised it between 1 and 7 p.m. So for daily coffee drinkers, the morning cup no longer moves cortisol, and the afternoon cup still does. The advice to wait 90 to 120 minutes after waking before the first coffee, which circulates on health podcasts, has no <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ensaio\">clinical trial</span> behind it.",
  "s03.p3": "Food also raises cortisol. Lunch causes a cortisol peak, larger in women after caffeine (Lovallo and colleagues, 2006), and continuous sampling every 10 minutes showed clear pulses at that hour (Bhake and colleagues, 2020). Eating less has an effect that depends on how strict the restriction is and that fades with time. Fasting raises cortisol strongly, while moderate calorie restriction does not, and the effect returns to normal within weeks (Nakamura and colleagues, 2016). In the same way, low-carbohydrate diets raised resting cortisol in the first three weeks (<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"efeito\">SMD</span> 0.41, a small to medium effect) and stopped raising it after that (Whittaker and Harris, 2022).",
  "s03.p4": "Exercise raises cortisol according to intensity. In 2008, Hill and colleagues had 12 active men exercise for 30 minutes at three intensities: 40%, 60% and 80% of their <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"vo2\">maximal oxygen uptake</span>. At rest, cortisol fell 6.6% over those 30 minutes, and at 40% it rose 5.7%, a value indistinguishable from zero. The rise became clear at 60%, with 39.9%, and at 80% it reached 83.1%, almost double the starting value. Part of that rise comes from more concentrated blood, because exercise draws fluid out of the plasma, and once corrected for this, light exercise lowered cortisol. The rise from a hard workout is part of the workout, because it releases fuel while the muscle works, and it fades within a few hours.",
  "s03.p5": "<span class=\"mark\">Short sleep leaves cortisol high at night, the hour when it should be falling.</span> In 1997, Rachel Leproult and colleagues measured the cortisol of young men for 32 hours straight and compared a normal night, a night in which the men slept only from 4 to 8 a.m., and a night without sleep. The next morning, cortisol did not change, but between 6 and 11 p.m. the following day it was 37% higher after the short night and 45% higher after the sleepless night, and the period of low cortisol at night began at least an hour later. In a 1999 experiment in which 11 young men spent six nights with 4 hours in bed, late-afternoon cortisol was also higher than during the recovery phase (Spiegel, Leproult and Van Cauter). Short sleep is common in Brazil, where 20.2% of adults in state capitals, one in five, sleep less than 6 hours a night, according to Vigitel 2025.",
  "s03.p6": "Changing the time of sleep shifts the whole day of cortisol. In 2001, Piérard and colleagues followed 27 soldiers on an eastbound flight that moved the clock forward 7 hours, the difference between São Paulo and Dubai, and in those on placebo, cortisol on waking stayed below normal from the second to the ninth day. Alcohol and smoking, by contrast, move cortisol at no fixed hour. Heavy drinking and withdrawal raise cortisol, but chronic use makes cortisol rise less in the face of stress, and smokers have higher cortisol throughout the day and on waking. The chart below places each habit at the hour it happens:",
  "c03.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What each habit does to the cortisol curve, hour by hour</b><span class=\"wdo\">Read the day from left to right. Each strip shows one habit at the hour it happens, and the colored area shows how much it raises cortisol.</span>",
  "c03.cap": "Baseline curve from Debono et al. (2009). Clow et al. (2004); Leproult et al. (2001) and (<em>Sleep</em>, 1997); Lovallo et al. (<em>Psychosom Med</em>, 2005 and 2006); Bhake et al. (2020); Hill et al. (<em>J Endocrinol Invest</em>, 2008), 12 men; Nakamura et al. (<em>Stress</em>, 2016); Whittaker and Harris (2022); Piérard et al. (2001). The studies are small and mostly in men. The half-life of cortisol, about 66 minutes (Kraan et al., 1997), sets how long each bump takes to fade.",
  "s03.p7": "Cortisol rose clearly in the harshest sleep experiments, such as the sleepless night and the six nights of 4 hours. A 2024 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"metaanalise\">meta-analysis</span> of 24 studies of acute sleep deprivation, however, found no consistent effect, and five nights of mild restriction did not change the waking rise in 111 men (Kontou and colleagues, 2022). Almost no trial has tested the reverse, whether sleeping more lowers evening cortisol.",
  "s03.p8": "A single workout raises cortisol, and weeks of regular exercise lower it. A <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"metarede\">network meta-analysis</span> of 44 trials in people with psychological distress found the largest drop in cortisol at around 530 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"met\">MET-minutes</span> of exercise a week, something like two and a half hours of brisk walking (Li and colleagues, 2025). Among types of exercise, yoga had the most reliable drop (SMD −0.59), and high-intensity interval training tended to raise cortisol. In a 2026 trial of 130 adults, a year of 150 minutes a week of aerobic exercise lowered the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cabelo\">cortisol stored in hair</span> (Gianaros and colleagues).",
  "s04.step": "04 · The stress that weighs most",
  "s04.h": "Cortisol surges when someone is judging you and the outcome is out of your control",
  "s04.p1": "In 1993, Clemens Kirschbaum, Karl Pirke and Dirk Hellhammer, from the German city of Trier, published <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tsst\">a standard way to induce stress in the laboratory</span>. The person has 10 minutes to prepare a speech and then spends 10 minutes speaking and doing mental arithmetic in front of a panel that does not smile. The test raises saliva cortisol to 2 to 4 times its resting value.",
  "s04.p2": "The version below lasts one minute, with a drawn panel and arithmetic you do for real, subtracting 13 starting from 1,022 and typing each result. When the minute is up, the cortisol curve of people who take the full test appears:",
  "c04a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">One minute in front of the panel</b><span class=\"wdo\">Subtract 13 starting from 1,022 and type each result as fast as you can; if you make a mistake, go back to 1,022. You can skip the task whenever you like.</span>",
  "c04a.cap": "Kirschbaum, Pirke and Hellhammer (<em>Neuropsychobiology</em>, 1993); timings from Dickerson and Kemeny (<em>Psychol Bull</em>, 2004). The shape of the curve between the published timings is illustrative. The task on this page is a short version and does not reproduce the test.",
  "s04.p3": "While the panel is watching and the tension is at its highest, cortisol has not yet started to rise. It peaks 21 to 40 minutes after the start and returns to its resting value between 41 and 60 minutes after the end (Dickerson and Kemeny, 2004).",
  "s04.p4": "In 2004, Sally Dickerson and Margaret Kemeny gathered 208 laboratory studies to find out what kind of situation raises cortisol. Sad or frightening films and noise did not change cortisol, while public speaking raised it, and public speaking with arithmetic raised it more. The situations that raised cortisol most combined two ingredients, someone evaluating the performance and an outcome the person cannot control. With both together, the typical person in the group ended up with more cortisol than 82% of the people in the control group, and with only one ingredient the share was close to 63%:",
  "c04b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What raises cortisol in a laboratory</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the bar in the bottom right corner, which combines the two ingredients, with the others, and then look at the bottom strip, with film and noise.</span>",
  "c04b.cap": "Dickerson and Kemeny (<em>Psychol Bull</em>, 2004), meta-analysis of 208 laboratory studies, mostly of young university students. The d measures the distance between groups in standard deviations; 0.2 is small and 0.8 is large. The percentages come from d by the standard conversion.",
  "s04.p5": "<span class=\"mark\">In the same studies, people reported the same level of stress with and without the two ingredients, and their cortisol was very different.</span> So cortisol reflects little of the nervousness a person feels and responds above all to being judged with no control over the outcome.",
  "s04.p6": "Those two ingredients come together in a job interview, a presentation to the boss, an exam and an argument in which the other person has the final say. In the laboratory studies, the time of day also changed the size of the effect, and studies run in the afternoon found larger effects (<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"efeito\">d = 0.46</span>) than those run in the morning (d = 0.14), because in the morning cortisol is already high and an extra rise shows up less.",
  "s05.step": "05 · One bad day against bad months",
  "s05.h": "On a hard day, the cortisol peak helps; months of stress leave cortisol high at night, and people who live that way get sick and die more often",
  "s05.p1": "On a hard day, the body’s reaction helps. Before a GRE math test, the American graduate admissions exam, Jeremy Jamieson and colleagues (2010) told one group of students that the body’s arousal improves performance. That group had more arousal, measured by <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"alfa\">an enzyme in saliva</span> that tracks <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"adrenalina\">adrenaline</span>, and scored higher in the laboratory and on the real exam, one to three months later. In critically ill patients, more cortisol also helped, according to a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cochrane\">Cochrane review</span> of four trials with 165 patients, in which <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hidrocortisona\">hydrocortisone</span> given soon after a trauma cut the risk of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"tept\">post-traumatic stress</span> to about one sixth (<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"rr\">RR</span> 0.17; Amos and colleagues, 2014).",
  "s05.p2": "Months of stress change the shape of the cortisol day. In a 2007 meta-analysis, Gregory Miller, Edith Chen and Eric Zhou showed that cortisol is high at the start of prolonged stress and falls as time goes on, and that stressors that are traumatic, uncontrollable or threatening to the body leave cortisol “high and flat”, with little difference between morning and evening. In people with post-traumatic stress, by contrast, cortisol tends to be lower. Hair, which records exposure over months, shows both directions, with 43% more cortisol in people under ongoing stress and 17% less in people with post-traumatic stress (Stalder and colleagues, 2017, 66 studies with 10,289 people). Adults who suffered abuse or adversity in childhood also respond to social stress with less cortisol (g = −0.39; Bunea and colleagues, 2017).",
  "s05.p3": "Work stress in Brazil shows up in the figures of the INSS, the national social security institute. Sick leave for mental disorders rose from about 283,000 in 2023 to about 472,000 in 2024, and sick leave for <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"burnout\">burnout</span> went from 823 in 2021 to 4,880 in 2024, from a small base. A review of 31 studies, however, found no difference in cortisol between people with burnout and controls (Danhof-Pont and colleagues, 2011), and burnout still has no marker in blood or saliva.",
  "s05.p4": "The largest study of the shape of the curve follows British civil servants, the Whitehall II study. On a working day, 4,047 of them, with an average age of 61, collected saliva six times: on waking, 30 minutes later, 2.5 hours later, 8 hours later, 12 hours later and at bedtime. Kumari and colleagues (2011) followed these people for 6.1 years, and 139 died in that period, about 3 in every 100.",
  "s05.p5": "<span class=\"mark\">Morning cortisol and the waking jump had no link to the deaths.</span> The link appeared in the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"inclinacao\">slope</span>, how far cortisol fell from waking to bedtime. For each <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dp\">standard deviation</span> of flatter curve, the risk of death from any cause was 30% higher (<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"hr\">HR</span> 1.30, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ic\">interval</span> 1.09 to 1.55), and the risk of cardiovascular death was 87% higher (HR 1.87, 1.32 to 2.64). One standard deviation roughly separates a median person from one among the 16% with the flattest curves.",
  "s05.p6": "In 2017, Emma Adam and colleagues pooled 80 studies, with 179 associations between the slope of the curve and health, totalling about 26,000 people. People whose cortisol fell less by evening had worse health in 10 of the 12 types of problem studied. The average correlation was small, <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"r\">r = 0.147</span>, and reached 0.288 for immunity and inflammation. The curves and bars below show the two studies:",
  "c05.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Two curves with the same morning, and the risk for people whose curve falls less</b><span class=\"wdo\">In the first panel, see what a morning test would read on each curve; in the second, compare the bars with the line at 1, which means equal risk.</span>",
  "c05.cap": "Kumari et al. (<em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em>, 2011), Whitehall II. Adam et al. (<em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em>, 2017), 80 studies and 179 associations. The times in panel 1 are the Whitehall II intervals applied to someone who wakes at 7 a.m. HR is the hazard ratio; 1 means equal risk.",
  "s05.p7": "Both studies are <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"observacional\">observational</span>, so a flat curve can be the result of an illness that was already there; Adam and colleagues discuss whether it is a marker or a mechanism. Another limit lies in Whitehall II, where cardiovascular deaths were few, 32. Even so, both studies show that the risk lies in how far cortisol falls by evening, and hardly at all in the morning value. A curve turns flat in two ways, with a low morning or a high evening, and the habits in section 03 act on both ends, because early light and waking lift the morning, and a short night lifts the evening.",
  "s06.step": "06 · When it becomes disease",
  "s06.h": "Truly high cortisol almost always comes from a drug, and too little cortisol is an emergency",
  "s06.p1": "In medicine, the round face of the “cortisol face” videos is called a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"facelua\">moon face</span>, and it is one of the signs of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"cushing\">Cushing’s syndrome</span>. The syndrome comes from an excess of cortisol over months, and the moon face appears together with other signs, the most common of which are fat on the back of the neck and above the collarbone, wide purple stretch marks, thin skin that bruises easily, thin arms and legs, and weak thighs. Christie Turin More, an endocrinologist at the University of Colorado, points out that “cortisol face” is not a medical term and that a puffy face without other signs is almost never Cushing’s.",
  "s06.p2": "Cushing’s that the body produces on its own, from a tumour in the pituitary or the adrenal gland, is rare. In western Sweden, between 2002 and 2017, there were 3.2 new cases per million inhabitants per year (Wengander and colleagues, 2019), about three a year in a city of one million people. The most common cause of Cushing’s is corticosteroid medication (Lacroix and colleagues, 2015), which many people take. In the United Kingdom, about 1% of the population is taking an oral corticosteroid at any given time (Fardet and colleagues, 2011), and in the United States the figure is 1.2%, of whom 28.8% have been taking it for five years or more (Overman and colleagues, 2013). Short prescriptions also carry risk. Among American adults with private health insurance, 21.1% received at least one short prescription, of less than 30 days, over three years, and in the following 30 days the risk of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"sepse\">sepsis</span> was 5.3 times higher, of blood clots 3.3 times and of fracture 1.9 times (Waljee and colleagues, 2017). The chart below places these groups among one million people:",
  "c06a.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">One million people, and who has abnormal cortisol</b><span class=\"wdo\">Each dot is one person (four on a phone), and the three loupes zoom in on the groups; look for the three Cushing’s dots.</span>",
  "c06a.cap": "Fardet, Petersen and Nazareth (<em>Rheumatology</em>, 2011); Overman et al. (<em>Arthritis Care Res</em>, 2013); Erichsen et al. (<em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em>, 2009); Wengander et al. (<em>Clin Endocrinol</em>, 2019). Prevalence (how many have it now) and incidence (how many new cases a year) are mixed on purpose and labelled.",
  "s06.p3": "A common 20 mg dose of prednisone is equivalent in potency to about 80 mg of hydrocortisone, the name of cortisol as a drug. <span class=\"mark\">That is eight times the cortisol the body makes in a day, every day of the treatment.</span>",
  "s06.p4": "The 43% extra in the hair of people under ongoing stress amounts to 1.4 times normal, far from the eightfold of that prednisone dose. In Cushing’s, free cortisol in the urine is usually several times above the upper limit, and the curve loses its midnight trough. That trough is what one of the screening tests looks for, cortisol in saliva collected between 11 p.m. and midnight, which has <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"sensibilidade\">sensitivity and specificity</span> above 90% for Cushing’s (Raff, 2009). The three portraits below compare the same person in three situations:",
  "c06b.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Cushing’s, a semester of stress and “cortisol face”</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare the three portraits of the same person and follow the lines to each sign; below, the ruler shows how much cortisol there is in each case.</span>",
  "c06b.cap": "Clinical signs of Cushing’s according to Lacroix et al. (<em>Lancet</em>, 2015). Stalder et al. (<em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em>, 2017). Equivalence of 5 mg of prednisone to 20 mg of hydrocortisone, standard table. Christie Turin More, CU Anschutz, 11/25/2024.",
  "s06.p5": "In 2026, the fad led more patients to ask for a cortisol test, often a blood test drawn at any hour, which is useless for screening (Shroff, <em>2 Minute Medicine</em>, 2026). Obesity, depression, alcohol and the contraceptive pill can raise test cortisol in someone who does not have Cushing’s. The Endocrine Society guideline says to rule out corticosteroid use first and to investigate only people with several signs that worsen over time (Nieman and colleagues, 2008).",
  "s06.p6": "Too little cortisol is more dangerous in the short term. In <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"addison\">Addison’s disease</span>, the immune system destroys the part of the adrenal gland that makes cortisol, and in Norway 144 people in every million have this autoimmune form of the disease (Erichsen and colleagues, 2009). Without cortisol, blood pressure drops when the person stands up, and a bout of diarrhoea or a fever can turn into an <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"crise\">adrenal crisis</span>, with low blood pressure and shock. Hahner and colleagues (2015) followed 423 patients for two years, all of them taught to raise their dose of medication when they fall ill, and recorded 8.3 crises per 100 patients per year, one crisis every 12 years for each patient. About 6% of those crises ended in death, and gut infection, fever and emotional stress each triggered 20% of them.",
  "s06.p7": "<span class=\"mark\">Corticosteroids, the drugs that most often cause high cortisol, are also the most common cause of truly low cortisol, when the person stops them all at once.</span> After weeks of medication, the hypothalamus and the pituitary stop stimulating the adrenals, because the drug already does cortisol’s job, and the adrenals stop producing. When the drug is stopped suddenly, they have not yet started working again. That is why anyone who has been taking a corticosteroid for weeks needs to talk to their doctor before stopping.",
  "s06.p8": "In the videos, too little cortisol appears as “<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"fadiga\">adrenal fatigue</span>”, the idea that years of stress wear out the gland. In 2016, the endocrinologists Flávio Cadegiani and Claudio Kater, of the Federal University of São Paulo, searched the literature for the condition and, out of 3,470 papers, found 58 studies that met the criteria, with results that were, in their words, almost systematically conflicting. The paper’s conclusion states that adrenal fatigue “is still a myth”. After their peak in March 2018, worldwide searches for “adrenal fatigue” fell to less than a third by 2026, while “cortisol face” and “cortisol belly” rose to the same volume. The wrong treatment can create the real disease, because hydrocortisone prescribed for “adrenal fatigue” switches off the adrenals, and on stopping it, the person can develop true <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"insuficiencia\">adrenal insufficiency</span>. In 2018, Akturk and colleagues tested the 12 most popular “adrenal support” supplements, and all of them contained thyroid hormone; 7 contained at least one steroid, and none of these hormones was declared on the label.",
  "s07.step": "07 · The meter",
  "s07.h": "Saliva cortisol varies more from one day to the next than from one person to another",
  "s07.p1": "The Hormometer has been on sale in the United States and Canada since September 2025. The user holds a cartridge in the mouth for 60 seconds, and the phone reads the result in about 20 minutes. Eli Health claims 97% accuracy compared with a laboratory method but does not publish the sample size, the reference method or the variation between measurements, and its own page says the device is a wellness product and does not replace clinical tests. Watches such as the Whoop give a stress score calculated from the heartbeat, without measuring cortisol.",
  "s07.p2": "Even a device with no error would show very different numbers from one morning to the next. The CIRCORT database pooled 104,623 saliva samples from 18,698 people (Miller and colleagues, 2016). In women aged 21 to 30, one hour after waking, the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"mediana\">median</span> was 7.5 <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"unidades\">nmol/L</span>, and 90% of values fell between 2.1 and 26.7 nmol/L, so that at the same hour the highest normal value is 13 times the lowest. Most of that variation came from the same person from one day to the next, with about 54% of the <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"variancia\">variance</span>, against 23% from differences between people (calculated from table 2 of the paper).",
  "s07.p3": "With that much day-to-day variation, a single sample at 8 a.m. had a <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"confiabilidade\">reliability</span> of between 0.18 and 0.47 from one visit to another, on a scale on which 1 would be the same answer every time (Golden and colleagues, 2011), and Hellhammer and colleagues (2007) calculated that 2 to 6 days of collection are needed to measure what belongs to the person. In the simulator below, the same healthy person collects saliva one hour after waking. Collect one sample, then more, and see where they land within the normal range:",
  "c07.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">The same person, one saliva sample per morning</b><span class=\"wdo\">Tap collect today to draw a morning, and after collecting at least ten, switch on the 6-day average.</span>",
  "c07.cap": "Miller et al. (<em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em>, 2016), CIRCORT; Golden et al. (<em>Eur J Epidemiol</em>, 2011); Hellhammer et al. (2007). The person is simulated from the CIRCORT variance components; the device’s 3% error is a generous assumption, unrelated to published data for any product.",
  "s07.p4": "The first dot seemed to say something about the person, but the following ones spread across much of the normal range, while the average of six mornings landed close to her median. The test that helps a doctor is saliva collected near midnight, when the trough should be low, in people with signs of Cushing’s (Raff, 2009). A meter used in the morning measures cortisol at the hour when it says the least about health, since in Whitehall II the morning value had no link to the deaths.",
  "s07.p5": "Demand for tests also grew in Brazil. In the SUS, the public health system, approved cortisol tests rose from 188,000 in 2019 to 510,000 in 2025, 2.7 times more, while tests for <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"estradiol\">estradiol</span>, another hormone, grew 2.3 times over the same period. The ratio between the two held steady at 0.27 to 0.28 from 2016 to 2022 and rose to 0.32 to 0.33 from 2024 on, an excess of 15% to 20% that coincides with the social-media wave, without proving that it came from it.",
  "s08.step": "08 · The tricks",
  "s08.h": "The tricks lower cortisol less than waking up raises it, and several things that raise it are good for you",
  "s08.p1": "<span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ashwagandha\">Ashwagandha</span>, a plant from traditional Indian medicine, is the most studied supplement against cortisol. The trials are small, mostly run in India, and many were funded by makers of patented extracts. A 2025 meta-analysis of 7 trials and 488 people found a drop of 1.16 µg/dL in morning blood cortisol and no change in the stress people reported, and two others found larger drops, of 2.58 µg/dL (2024) and 3.27 µg/dL (2026). Between the morning peak and midnight, cortisol falls by about 13.5 µg/dL on its own, and the drop attributed to the plant ranges from a twelfth to a quarter of that. A 2026 meta-analysis of 23 trials found a standardized effect of −1.18, larger than that of many drugs, but large effects in small trials tend to shrink when bigger studies arrive.",
  "s08.p2": "The plant also carries a risk to the liver. Björnsson and colleagues (2020) described five cases of liver injury linked to it, and 2026 reviews collect 26 to 37 cases, some requiring a transplant. Denmark banned ashwagandha in supplements in 2023. <span class=\"mark\">In Brazil, it has been banned as a supplement since 2022, and Google searches for it doubled between 2023 and 2025.</span> On October 21, 2026, Anvisa, the Brazilian health regulator, reaffirmed that its manufacture, sale, distribution and use are banned in the country.",
  "s08.p3": "The effect of meditation depends on where cortisol is measured. In the meta-analysis by Koncz and colleagues (2021), meditation lowered blood cortisol (g = 0.62, 10 studies, 395 people), almost always measured in a single morning sample, and the effect appeared mainly in people with some illness. In saliva, by contrast, in 21 studies with 1,163 people, the effect was g = 0.18 and could not be distinguished from zero. When the authors corrected for <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"vies\">publication bias</span>, which comes from studies with no result that never get published, the effect in saliva became −0.10.",
  "s08.p4": "Laughter lowered cortisol by 31.9% on average, in 8 studies with 315 people, with small samples and varied collection times (Kramer and Leitão, 2023). A review by Tiffany Field’s group reported that massage lowers cortisol by 31%, comparing before and after in the same group. A control group’s cortisol also falls from the start to the end of a session, and when Moyer and colleagues (2011) compared massage with control groups, in 18 studies with 704 people, the effect was very small and not significant. In 138 sedentary, overweight adults, 2.5 g of <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"omega\">omega-3</span> a day for four months left cortisol 19% lower during a laboratory stress test (Madison and colleagues, 2021), while for magnesium none of the reviews had cortisol as the main outcome (Boyle and colleagues, 2017).",
  "s08.p5": "The “cortisol cocktail” has never been tested, and the same drink has been sold for two opposite problems. It was born in 2023 as the “adrenal cocktail”, sold to replace what a “tired” adrenal gland would fail to make, and in 2024 it was renamed to lower cortisol. A glass has about 16 g of sugar, a third of the recommended daily limit, and a quarter of the daily limit for salt (Larkin, <em>The Conversation</em>, 2026).",
  "s08.p6": "Several things from section 03, such as bright morning light, hard exercise and waking itself, raise cortisol and are good for you. The ruler below puts on one scale everything that has a comparable number, with the waking rise as the reference:",
  "c08.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">What raises and what lowers cortisol, on the same ruler</b><span class=\"wdo\">Compare each row with the grey band of the waking rise, which repeats every day. Effects that lower cortisol sit left of zero, and those that raise it sit to the right.</span>",
  "c08.cap": "Kramer and Leitão (<em>PLOS ONE</em>, 2023); Madison et al. (<em>Mol Psychiatry</em>, 2021); Field et al. (2005); Moyer et al. (2011); Hill et al. (2008); Leproult et al. (1997; 2001); Kirschbaum et al. (1993); Clow et al. (2004); ashwagandha meta-analyses from 2024, 2025 and 2026; Koncz et al. (<em>Health Psychol Rev</em>, 2021). The percentages come from different measures (blood, saliva, different times of day); the ruler compares orders of magnitude.",
  "s08.p7": "Even when a treatment works, cortisol may not change. In 12 trials with 428 people with post-traumatic stress, for example, psychotherapy improved symptoms and left cortisol unchanged (g = −0.07; Schumacher and colleagues, 2018).",
  "s09.step": "09 · What to do",
  "s09.h": "Almost no one needs to lower morning cortisol; what goes with disease is cortisol that stays high at night",
  "s09.p1": "For a healthy person, lowering morning cortisol would make the morning look more like the night, and that curve that falls little is the one the studies link to more disease. The habits that most affect evening cortisol are well known, and the first is sleep. Sleeping enough, on a regular schedule, lets cortisol fall at the right hour, while a short night leaves it 37% higher between 6 and 11 p.m. the next day. Bright light between 5 and 8 a.m. raises the peak, and so the fall toward night becomes larger. For daily coffee drinkers, the time of the cup decides the effect, because the 9 a.m. cup does not move cortisol and the early-afternoon cup still does. Regular, moderate exercise, around two and a half hours of brisk walking a week, goes with lower cortisol over months.",
  "s09.p2": "Having someone close by also lowers cortisol. In 37 men, the presence of a friend lowered cortisol during a stress test, and a friend combined with <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"ocitocina\">oxytocin</span> lowered it more (Heinrichs and colleagues, 2003). In 67 women, 10 minutes of massage from a partner before the test lowered cortisol and heart rate, while verbal support alone did not (Ditzen and colleagues, 2007). The stress that raises cortisol most combines judgement and lack of control (section 04), so work situations with both are natural candidates for change. This advice has been known for a long time, and James Findling, an endocrinologist at the Medical College of Wisconsin, told the AP in February 2026 that sleeping, exercising and managing stress are “nothing new”.",
  "s09.p3": "High cortisol that needs a doctor comes with several signs that worsen over months, such as a face that rounds out along with purple stretch marks, thin skin and weak legs, and it is investigated with late-night saliva, 24-hour urine or <span class=\"gl\" data-gl=\"dexametasona\">dexamethasone</span>. Low cortisol that needs a doctor, by contrast, appears in people with Addison’s or who have stopped a corticosteroid, with blood pressure dropping on standing and crises during infections.",
  "s09.p4": "The chart below shows the measured curve and, dashed, the curve you drew in section 02, if you drew one. Drag the day’s stamps to the hour they happen and watch the meter, which shows how far cortisol falls from waking to bedtime. Try to build a day in which it barely falls:",
  "c09.tag": "<b class=\"wt\">Build a day and see how far cortisol falls by night</b><span class=\"wdo\">Drag the stamps to the hour they happen and watch the meter, which shows how far cortisol falls from waking to bedtime. Try to make it barely fall.</span>",
  "c09.cap": "Illustrative model. The baseline curve is from Debono et al. (2009); the effects are the published averages from the sources cited on each stamp, added together simply, and the real body does not add up so neatly.",
  "s09.p5": "Almost everything that happens during the day makes a bump that fades within an hour or two, because half the cortisol in the blood is gone in a little over an hour. Because of that, the curve only turns flat when something lifts the evening or lowers the morning, such as a short night, afternoon coffee, the jet lag of an eastbound flight or a morning without light.",
  "s09.p6": "If it were measured, the slow life of the “low cortisol” meme, with unhurried coffee and calm music, would have cortisol high at 8 a.m., like that of any healthy person, and low at midnight.",
  "m.step": "Note on method",
  "m.l1": "The social-media fad opens the essay and returns in sections 06 to 09, and each claim from the videos cited in the text was checked against published studies.",
  "m.l2": "The cortisol numbers come from three places that cannot be compared directly: blood (total cortisol, in µg/dL), saliva (free cortisol only, in nmol/L) and hair (exposure over months). Each chart uses only one of these measures and names it on the axis. The ruler in section 08 combines percentages from different measures and is meant for comparing orders of magnitude.",
  "m.l3": "The day curve joins the five numbers published by Debono and colleagues (2009) with a smooth line, which hides the pulses of about an hour. In the habit charts of sections 03 and 09, effects with no published number are drawn dashed and labelled as illustrative shapes.",
  "m.l4": "The models of the panel (section 04), the saliva samples (section 07) and the day you build (section 09) use published numbers joined by shapes chosen to fit on screen. They show the shape of the phenomenon and do not predict anyone’s cortisol. The person in the saliva samples is simulated with the CIRCORT split of variation between days and between people, calculated from table 2 of the paper.",
  "m.l5": "Some numbers in the text are calculations from the published data: the 82% and 63% derived from Dickerson and Kemeny’s d; one standard deviation read as the distance to the 16% with the flattest curves; 139 deaths among 4,047 as about 3 in 100; one Addison’s crisis every 12 years per patient; 20 mg of prednisone as about eight times a day’s production; the ashwagandha drop as a fraction of the daily fall of 13.5 µg/dL; and the Whitehall II times applied to someone who wakes at 7 a.m.",
  "m.l6": "The studies of sleep, single workouts and jet lag are small and mostly in men only. The studies of cortisol’s fall and health are observational, so they show that the two go together without proving that one causes the other.",
  "m.l7": "Some numbers come from a paper’s abstract or from news reports, without a reading of the full text. Among them are the effect of morning light, the cases of liver injury linked to ashwagandha, the INSS sick-leave figures and the sugar and salt in the cortisol cocktail. When the source gives no value, the chart draws only the shape and says so.",
  "m.l8": "Google Trends: the index is relative within each query and was collected on 11/12/2026; different panels cannot be compared. SUS: SIA/SUS data queried through TabNet; August 2023 has a processing error and was left out of the ratio between cortisol and estradiol.",
  "m.l9": "This essay is not medical advice, and anyone taking a corticosteroid should not stop without talking to their doctor.",
  "f.step": "Main sources",
  "f.refs": "\n  <b>Starting point.</b> Google Trends, queries of 11/12/2026. Know Your Meme, “Low Cortisol Dance”. Eli Health, Hormometer science page. AP, “Do you need to control your cortisol? Probably not, doctors say”, 2/2/2026. CU Anschutz, “Cortisol face”, 11/25/2024. Shroff, <em>2 Minute Medicine</em>, 9/4/2026. Larkin, <em>The Conversation</em>/PsyPost, 8/25/2026.<br>\n  <b>Function and curve.</b> Esteban et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 1991. Kraan et al., 1997. Debono et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 2009. Spiga et al., <em>Compr Physiol</em> 2014. Lupien et al., <em>Brain Cogn</em> 2007. Adam et al., <em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em> 2017. Husebye et al., <em>Lancet</em> 2021. Hench and cortisone, Mayo Clinic historical archive. Stalder et al., <em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em> 2016. Clow et al., <em>Stress</em> 2004. Wüst et al., 2000. Fries et al., 2009.<br>\n  <b>Lifestyle.</b> Leproult et al., 2001 (light). Lovallo et al., <em>Psychosom Med</em> 2005 and 2006. Bhake et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 2020. Nakamura et al., <em>Stress</em> 2016. Whittaker & Harris, 2022. Hill et al., <em>J Endocrinol Invest</em> 2008. Li et al., 2025 (network meta-analysis, 44 trials). Gianaros et al., <em>J Sport Health Sci</em> 2026. Leproult et al., <em>Sleep</em> 1997. Spiegel, Leproult & Van Cauter, <em>Lancet</em> 1999. Kontou et al., 2022. Piérard et al., 2001. Vigitel 2025. Alcohol, 2023 meta-analysis of 46 studies. Steptoe and Ussher, cortisol in smokers.<br>\n  <b>Stress.</b> Kirschbaum, Pirke & Hellhammer, <em>Neuropsychobiology</em> 1993. Dickerson & Kemeny, <em>Psychol Bull</em> 2004. Jamieson et al., <em>J Exp Soc Psychol</em> 2010. Miller, Chen & Zhou, <em>Psychol Bull</em> 2007. Stalder et al., <em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em> 2017 (hair). Bunea et al., <em>Transl Psychiatry</em> 2017. Amos et al., Cochrane 2014. Danhof-Pont et al., <em>J Psychosom Res</em> 2011. Kumari et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 2011 (Whitehall II). INSS, sick leave 2021 to 2024, via news reports.<br>\n  <b>Disease.</b> Nieman et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 2008 (guideline). Raff, 2009. Lacroix et al., <em>Lancet</em> 2015. Wengander et al., <em>Clin Endocrinol</em> 2019. Fardet, Petersen & Nazareth, 2011. Overman et al., 2013. Waljee et al., <em>BMJ</em> 2017. Erichsen et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 2009. Hahner et al., <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab</em> 2015. Cadegiani & Kater, <em>BMC Endocr Disord</em> 2016. Akturk et al., <em>Mayo Clin Proc</em> 2018.<br>\n  <b>Measurement.</b> Miller et al., <em>Psychoneuroendocrinology</em> 2016 (CIRCORT). Golden et al., <em>Eur J Epidemiol</em> 2011. Hellhammer et al., 2007. SIA/SUS, TabNet/DATASUS, query of 11/12/2026.<br>\n  <b>Interventions.</b> Koncz et al., <em>Health Psychol Rev</em> 2021. Kramer & Leitão, <em>PLOS ONE</em> 2023. Field et al., <em>Int J Neurosci</em> 2005. Moyer et al., <em>J Bodyw Mov Ther</em> 2011. Madison et al., <em>Mol Psychiatry</em> 2021. Boyle et al., <em>Nutrients</em> 2017. Heinrichs et al., 2003. Ditzen et al., 2007. Schumacher et al., 2018. Ashwagandha meta-analyses: <em>Explore</em> 2024 (PMID 39348746), <em>Nutrition and Health</em> 2025 (PMID 40746175), <em>Planta Medica</em> 2026 (PMID 41740946), <em>JoVE</em> 2026 (PMID 42858054). Björnsson et al., <em>Liver International</em> 2020. Anvisa, notice of 10/21/2026. NutraIngredients, regulatory timeline, 7/6/2026.\n  ",
  "f.foot": "How to lower your cortisol (and whether you should) · interactive essay · November 2026"
};
